        "text": "\n000\nFXUS62 KMFL 221124\nAFDMFL\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Miami FL\n624 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\n...New AVIATION...\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\nUpdated at 618 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\n  - Elevated risk of rip currents today for the Palm Beaches.\n\n  - Patchy fog early this morning over interior South FL.\n\n  - Minor coastal flooding possible during high tide beginning\n    Tuesday.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM...\n(Today through Monday)\nIssued at 118 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nAfter an eventful Saturday across South FL, it should be a quieter \ncouple of days as the shortwave axis continues to move well east of \nthe area and the weak frontal boundary settles off the southeast FL \ncoast. Some residual moisture may keep a few showers in near the SE \nFL coast today, with dry conditions expected on Monday. Patchy fog \nwill again be possible early this morning over interior portions of \nSouth FL. Light westerly winds are expected today and then winds \nshould turn northerly on Monday. \n\nHigh temps today will be in the low to middle 80s. Low temps tonight \nwill fall into the 60s. Cooler temps are expected on Monday with \nafternoon highs in the upper 70s to lower 80s.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM...\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 118 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nThe next system to impact our region will be a quick moving \nshortwave ejecting out of the SW US towards the Gulf on Tuesday and \nthen moving towards the eastern Gulf on Wednesday. Another frontal \nboundary and weak low will approach the area by mid week. Increased \nmoisture will result in scattered showers and thunderstorms on \nTuesday and Wednesday, with Wednesday looking to be the most active \nday of the week. PWAT values on Wednesday increase to 1.8-2.0 inches \nand depending on the track of the low some locally heavy rain may \noccur in portions of the area, especially SE FL. The shortwave axis \nswings through the area by late Wednesday into early Thursday which \nwill kick the frontal boundary well off to our east, and drier air \nwill return to the area for the end of the work week. \n\nTemperatures through the end of the work week and into the beginning \nof next weekend will be fairly consistent with highs in the upper \n70s to lower 80s each day, and low temps generally in the 60s.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...\n(12Z TAFS) \nIssued at 620 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nVFR is now establishing at all terminals and expected to prevail\nduring the next 24 hours. However, some lingering bkn010 cigs may\nkeep potential for brief MVFR over a few Atlantic terminals \nthrough 14Z. L/V flow will become westerly 5-10 kts later this \nmorning and through the afternoon hours, then again L/V after 00Z.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nIssued at 118 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nGentle to moderate westerly breeze today becoming northerly on \nMonday. Seas 2 ft or less in the Gulf and 1-3 ft in the Atlantic. A \nfew thunderstorms possible in the Atlantic today which may result in \nlocally hazardous winds and seas. A system crossing the area mid to \nlate week may bring a brief period of hazardous seas to the Atlantic \nwaters.\n\n&&\n\n.BEACHES...\nIssued at 118 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nLingering swell will result in an elevated risk of rip currents \ntoday and Monday for the Palm Beaches.\n\nMinor coastal flooding is possible beginning with Tuesday\nmorning's high tide cycle along both the Atlantic and Gulf coasts. \n\n&&\n\n.PRELIMINARY POINT TEMPS/POPS...\nMiami            84  68  80  69 /  10   0   0  10 \nWest Kendall     84  67  80  69 /  10   0   0  10 \nOpa-Locka        85  67  80  68 /  10   0   0  10 \nHomestead        85  67  81  69 /  10   0   0  10 \nFort Lauderdale  84  66  79  68 /  10   0   0  10 \nN Ft Lauderdale  83  66  79  68 /  10   0   0  10 \nPembroke Pines   85  65  81  67 /  10   0   0  10 \nWest Palm Beach  82  65  78  67 /  10   0   0  10 \nBoca Raton       83  66  79  70 /  10   0   0  10 \nNaples           80  64  78  66 /   0   0   0   0 \n\n&&\n\n.MFL WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nFL...None.\nAM...None.\nGM...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...CMF\nLONG TERM....CMF\nAVIATION...17\n"
      },
